  "prediction": "**War-Related and International Emergency Funding: A Review of Congressional Oversight Issues**\n\nSince the 9/11 terrorist attacks, Congress has appropriated approximately $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority for war-related and international emergency or contingency-designated funding. This report provides an overview of the funding designated for emergencies or Overseas Contingency Operations/Global War on Terrorism (OCO/GWOT) since FY2001.\n\n**Funding Breakdown**\n\nThe majority of the funding, $1.8 trillion, has been allocated to the Department of Defense (DOD), with $154 billion going to the Department of State and U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID), and $3 billion to the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Coast Guard.\n\n**OCO Funding Trends**\n\nDOD's OCO funding has averaged 17% of the department's total budget authority since FY2001. The funding has peaked in FY2008, with $187 billion allocated, and has decreased to $68.8 billion in FY2019. The majority of OCO funding has been used for Operation and Maintenance (O&M) costs, which pay for the operating costs of the military.\n\n**Controversies and Criticisms**\n\nThe use of OCO funding has been criticized for being a \"slush fund\" for military and foreign affairs spending unrelated to contingency operations. Critics argue that the funding has been used for base-budget requirements, such as purchasing F-35 fighter jets, and that the criteria for determining which activities are eligible for OCO funding are outdated and not always used.\n\n**Future of OCO Funding**\n\nThe future of OCO funding is uncertain, with some lawmakers recommending that Congress eliminate the BCA and use OCO funding to increase the defense budget. Others have suggested that Congress should repeal the BCA and use OCO funding to meet current or revised discretionary defense spending caps. The Trump Administration has proposed returning to OCO's original purpose by shifting certain costs funded in OCO to the base budget where they belong.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe use of OCO funding has been a contentious issue in Congress, with some lawmakers arguing that it has been used for base-budget requirements and others arguing that it is necessary to fund war-related activities. The future of OCO funding will depend on the decisions made by Congress and the Administration in the coming years.",
